steps.message:
The issue was that `Progress.stop()` would print a blank line on non-interactive consoles even when the progress display was disabled (`disabled=True`). This happened because `Progress.stop()` called `self.console.print()` without checking `self.disable`, whereas `Progress.start()` correctly checked `self.disable` before starting the live display.

I have updated `rich/progress.py` to wrap the contents of the `stop()` method in an `if not self.disable:` block, making it symmetric to the `start()` method and preventing the extraneous blank line when progress is disabled.

```python
    def stop(self) -> None:
        """Stop the progress display."""
        if not self.disable:
            self.live.stop()
            if not self.console.is_interactive and not self.console.is_jupyter:
                self.console.print()
